{ % vim: set ft=djot : % }

# Contributing to djotls

Thank you for your interest in contributing to djotls!

## Build and test

Build and installation commands are in the [README](README.dj#Build).
For development, `cargo build` also builds both binaries in debug mode.

### Test

Initialize the pinned reference submodules before running the full
suite:

``` bash
git submodule update --init --recursive
DJOTLS_REQUIRE_CONFORMANCE=1 cargo test --release
```

Parser conformance needs Node.js and, when the reference CLI needs
rebuilding, npm. The harness builds `third_party/djot.js/lib/cli.js`
automatically and may install its locked dependencies. Without
`DJOTLS_REQUIRE_CONFORMANCE=1`, a missing reference can cause
conformance tests to be skipped; CI requires it.
`DJOTLS_SKIP_JS_BUILD=1` disables automatic reference builds and can be
used with an already built reference. Neither Rust binary needs
JavaScript at runtime. Workspace protocol tests need Git on `PATH` and
use temporary repositories.

Formatter cases pair `tests/<name>.in` with `tests/<name>.out`; every
`.out` is also checked for idempotency. Add `{ % @columns: N % }` to an
input to set its test width (default 72). The README and this guide are
also formatter fixtures through symlinks in `tests/`, so their `.dj`
sources must be idempotent under `djotfmt`. Parser cases come from the
pinned djot.js suite. The same harness runs `formatter-semantics::*`
regressions at widths 24 and 72: it compares Djot.js ASTs before and
after formatting, and checks idempotency. These cases cover comments,
attributes, divs, link targets, ordered lists, code blocks and tables
inside lists. The comparison ignores ordinary text wrapping and list
tightness, while retaining attributes, destinations, list types and
starting ordinals, code contents and block structure.

Unit and stdio tests cover analysis, text
coordinates, navigation, completion, diagnostics, references, rename and
file moves.

Run `cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`
before submitting changes. Opt-in allocation and Linux process probes
are in [memory measurement](docs/memory.dj).

## Documentation

Maintain project documentation directly in `.dj` format. `AGENTS.md` is
kept in Markdown so Codex discovers the repository instructions
automatically.
There are no generated Markdown copies to update. Format the README and
this
guide before running their idempotency tests:

``` bash
cargo run -r -- -i README.dj CONTRIBUTING.dj
```

CLI help is plain text embedded by Clap. Keep `docs/long_about.txt` and
`docs/after_help.txt` in `.txt` format; these files do not use Djot
markup.

Multiline block comments use block-attribute syntax: indent every
continuation line, including a closing `% }` on its own line, relative
to the opening line. In a list or quote, keep this extra indentation
after the container prefix.

Keep user-facing behavior and limitations in the guides. Put design
reasons
beside the code that needs them; link longer investigations from those
comments.
The `third_party/` and `.format/` submodules maintain their own
documentation
in their upstream formats. License texts retain their standard
filenames.

## Releases

The `package.version` in `Cargo.toml` drives stable and prerelease
versions. Update it and `Cargo.lock` together, then merge to `master`.
After the same commit passes the `pass` job, CI calls
`.github/workflows/cd.yml`. The shared GoReleaser action validates the
version, creates and pushes `v<version>`, publishes GitHub Release
archives, and publishes the crate to crates.io using Trusted Publishing.

Each archive includes `djotfmt` and `djotls`. The configured targets are
Linux and macOS, on x86_64 and aarch64. The project installs
`cargo-zigbuild` in the `before.hooks` of `.goreleaser.yaml`.

For an RC, use a version such as `0.1.1-rc.1`. Tags are created
automatically for both stable and RC versions; `release.prerelease:
auto` marks GitHub prereleases. Release jobs run one at a time. If
publication fails after tagging, use {*Re-run failed jobs*} on the
original CI run to retry the same commit. Versions already published to
crates.io are skipped. PR checks, scheduled checks and manual CI runs do
not publish.

### Trusted Publisher setup

Configure a Trusted Publisher for the `djotls` crate on crates.io:

- Repository owner: `black-desk`

- Repository name: `djotls`

- Workflow filename: `ci.yml`

- Environment: leave unset

No long-lived crates.io token is needed. The OIDC caller identity is
explained beside the release job in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`.

## Debug

djotfmt uses the `log` crate for logging. You can increase the verbosity
level with the `-v` flag to see more detailed information about the
formatting process.

- {_(default)_} Warn: Only warnings and errors.

- `-v` Info: General informational messages.

- `-vv` Debug: Show parser event annotations and the corresponding
  source text.

- `-vvv` Trace: Show internal rendering state: pending words, pending
  lines and wrapping.

For example, to debug the formatting of a file with full trace output:

``` bash
cargo run -- -vvv input.dj
```

At `Debug` level, each event is logged as its annotation and source
slice, for example `Event: +para "h"` or `Event: str "hello world"`. At
`Trace` level, the formatter also logs pending words, word commits,
pending lines and wrapping; the CLI logs input, output and swap-file
operations.
